---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";

const sections = [
  { id: "how", title: "How it works" },
  { id: "connect", title: "Connect your bot" },
  { id: "mention", title: "Mention your bot" },
  { id: "in-slack", title: "What you see in Slack" },
  { id: "approvals", title: "Approve from Slack" },
  { id: "desktop", title: "Find the conversation in OpenOrc" },
  { id: "limits", title: "Limits" },
  { id: "next-steps", title: "Next steps" },
];
---

<DocsLayout slug="slack" sections={sections}>
  <section aria-labelledby="how">
    <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
    <p>
      You create your own Slack app, a personal bot, and connect it to OpenOrc. When you mention the bot in a channel, an agent runs on your computer with your agent sign-ins, and the Slack thread shows its progress, its reply, and
      its permission requests. OpenOrc connects through Slack's Socket Mode, an outgoing connection from your computer, so there's no server to host. Each person creates a separate bot.
    </p>
    <Callout>
      For a group that shares one bot, <strong>Connect Slack</strong> also offers <strong>Team relay · advanced</strong>. One computer holds the Slack connection, and each teammate's desktop connects to it through an SSH tunnel
      to receive that teammate's requests.
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="connect">
    <h2 id="connect">Connect your bot</h2>
    <p>You need a signed-in agent (see <strong>Settings</strong> → <strong>Connections</strong>). Your Slack workspace may need an admin to approve the new app.</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Open <strong>Settings</strong> → <strong>Slack</strong>. Leave <strong>Connect Slack</strong> set to <strong>Personal bot · no server needed</strong>.</li>
      <li>
        Under <strong>Set up your Slack app</strong>, enter an <strong>App name</strong> and click <strong>Create Slack app</strong>. In Slack, choose your workspace, review the prefilled settings, and create the app. If the form
        isn't prefilled, click <strong>Copy manifest</strong> and create the app from a manifest.
      </li>
      <li>In the app's settings in Slack, open OAuth &amp; Permissions, click Install to Workspace, and copy the Bot User OAuth Token (<code>xoxb-…</code>).</li>
      <li>Open Basic Information → App-Level Tokens → Generate Token and Scopes, add <code>connections:write</code>, and copy the token (<code>xapp-…</code>). Both tokens must come from the same app.</li>
      <li>In Slack, open your own profile, then More → Copy member ID.</li>
      <li>In OpenOrc, paste the tokens into <strong>Bot token</strong> and <strong>App token</strong>, and the ID into <strong>Your Slack member ID</strong>.</li>
      <li>Fill in <strong>Execution defaults</strong>, described in the table below.</li>
      <li>Click <strong>Connect and check</strong>. OpenOrc checks the tokens and that the ID belongs to an active person in the bot's workspace, then connects. The badge reads <strong>Connected</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Setting</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Workspace entrypoint</strong></td>
            <td>The folder new Slack conversations start in. The agent can also move to a project you've added, or to a folder up to three levels inside this one. <strong>Choose folder</strong> changes it.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Default coding agent</strong>, <strong>Default model</strong></td>
            <td>What a new Slack conversation starts with. The agent is Codex unless you change it; a blank model means the agent's default.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Mode</strong></td>
            <td>
              Applies to every Slack turn, existing threads included, and a stricter mode set on the conversation still wins. The default is <strong>Review everything</strong>, which Codex calls <strong>Review changes</strong>. OpenCode
              offers only <strong>Plan</strong> and <strong>Autonomous</strong> here.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>While Slack is connected, these fields are locked. Click <strong>Disconnect Slack</strong> to change them.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mention">
    <h2 id="mention">Mention your bot</h2>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Invite the bot to a channel. Private channels need an invite too.</li>
      <li>From the Slack account you connected, mention the bot with a request, such as <code>@your-bot what does this project do?</code></li>
      <li>To continue, reply in the same Slack thread. You don't need to mention the bot again.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The bot answers "Request received for your OpenOrc desktop." The agent gets the whole Slack thread as context, including messages from before the mention.</p>
    <p>
      To switch to another model, a higher reasoning effort, a different folder, or one of your <a href={docHref("orclings")}>Orclings</a>, ask in plain words. The agent switches after its current turn and carries on with the same
      request, up to four switches per request.
    </p>
    <p>Your computer handles one Slack request at a time. Up to 20 replies in the same Slack thread wait their turn. A mention in another thread gets a reply asking you to wait for the current request.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="in-slack">
    <h2 id="in-slack">What you see in Slack</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A progress message that OpenOrc edits as the agent works, at most every 2 seconds, with a status line such as <code>Working · &lt;model&gt; · &lt;tool&gt;</code> and the latest text of the reply.</li>
      <li>The final reply, which replaces the progress message. Slack shows up to 11,500 characters of it; the conversation in OpenOrc has all of it.</li>
      <li>Notices, for example when the agent is waiting for you or a request failed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every message mentions you. The agent's reasoning and tool output aren't posted, and mentions in its text are escaped, so it can't ping anyone. Before posting, OpenOrc also hides well-known secrets, such as API keys,
      on a best-effort basis.
    </p>
    <Callout>Everyone in the channel can read the replies and the details of permission requests. Use a private channel for private work.</Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="approvals">
    <h2 id="approvals">Approve from Slack</h2>
    <p>
      When the agent needs permission, OpenOrc posts the request in the Slack thread with <strong>Allow once</strong> and <strong>Deny</strong> buttons. A long request is split across several messages, and the buttons come with the
      last one.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Only you can decide. A click counts only from the member ID you connected, on the latest request message.</li>
      <li>Buttons expire after 10 minutes, and restarting OpenOrc retires them.</li>
      <li>If you answer on the desktop instead, the Slack message changes to say the request is no longer pending.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Questions that need a written answer aren't posted. Slack says the agent is waiting, and you answer in OpenOrc.</p>
    <p>
      Other people in the channel can add context: their messages and images in the thread reach the agent, which is told not to act on their instructions. Their mentions don't start work on your computer, and
      they can't press your buttons.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="desktop">
    <h2 id="desktop">Find the conversation in OpenOrc</h2>
    <p>
      Each Slack thread becomes a conversation in the <strong>Workspace</strong> group of the sidebar, titled "Slack:" and the start of your first message. It has the full record, including other people's messages, the agent's
      steps, and complete replies. A new Slack conversation doesn't change what your window shows.
    </p>
    <p>Stop a run or answer a written question on the desktop. If you're running a turn there yourself, the bot asks you to wait until it finishes.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="limits">
    <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Slack reaches your agent only while OpenOrc is open and connected and your computer is awake. Nothing waits on a server.</li>
      <li>After OpenOrc restarts, click <strong>Connect and check</strong> again. A connection that drops while OpenOrc is open, for example while your computer sleeps, comes back by itself.</li>
      <li>OpenOrc doesn't go back for mentions it missed while offline. Mention the bot again; earlier messages reach the agent as part of the thread.</li>
      <li>Disconnecting doesn't stop a running agent; its reply is posted once you reconnect. Quitting OpenOrc ends the request, and after you reconnect the Slack thread gets a note that it was interrupted.</li>
      <li>The bot works in channels only. It ignores direct messages and replies sent with Also send to channel.</li>
      <li>A request can be up to 20,000 characters. PNG, JPEG, GIF, and WebP images up to 20 MB reach the agent; other files don't.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "permissions" }, { slug: "orclings" }, { slug: "security" }]} columns={3} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
